# Database Modeling & Migrations

## Schema design: standard normalized design, idiomatic per ORM/framework

No personal override on naming (singular vs plural tables), ID strategy
(UUID vs serial/auto-increment), or timestamp columns — follow whatever
the ORM/framework's own convention is (Prisma, Drizzle, Django, Rails,
etc. each have their own idiom; don't fight it). Normalize by default;
denormalize only when a measured read pattern justifies it (ties to
`warisskill-performance-optimization`'s measure-first rule — don't
denormalize speculatively).

## Migration rigor: scaled to project stage

Two modes, decided by whether the project has real production traffic
depending on it:

**Pre-launch / no real users yet**: simple, direct migrations are fine.
`ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL` without a default, a
non-concurrent index, a straight `UPDATE` for a data fix — none of this
needs zero-downtime ceremony when there's no traffic to disrupt. Optimize
for moving fast.

**Post-launch / real production traffic**: full production-safe
discipline, no exceptions:

- Never a full table lock on a large table — use concurrent operations.
- New columns are nullable or have a default; never `NOT NULL` with no
  default on an existing table (forces a full rewrite/lock).
- Indexes created `CONCURRENTLY`, not inline, on existing tables.
- Schema and data migrations are separate — never mix DDL and DML in one
  migration.
- Renames use the expand-contract pattern (add new → backfill → dual-write
  in app code → drop old), never a direct rename.
- Large backfills run batched with progress tracking, not one giant
  transaction.
- Migrations are forward-only once deployed — a bad migration gets fixed
  by a new migration, never edited after it's run in production.

The switch from mode one to mode two is itself worth noticing and flagging
— once a project has real users, say so explicitly rather than silently
continuing with pre-launch habits.

## Migration safety checklist (post-launch mode)

- [ ] Migration has both UP and DOWN, or is explicitly marked irreversible
- [ ] No full-table locks on large tables
- [ ] New columns nullable or defaulted
- [ ] Indexes added concurrently
- [ ] Data backfill separated from schema change
- [ ] Rollback plan documented

## Limitations

- This is schema/migration discipline, not database choice or query
  optimization — see `warisskill-performance-optimization` for indexing
  driven by measured query performance, and
  `warisskill-system-design-decision-making` for whether a database
  engine choice itself warrants an ADR.
